WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.066
It was a normal day for Leo.

00:00:02.066 --> 00:00:04.141
His shop was ready, but customers kept

00:00:04.141 --> 00:00:05.049
walking.

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.059
For days, he faced the same question:

00:00:07.059 --> 00:00:09.005
who should he reach, and what should

00:00:09.005 --> 00:00:09.912
he offer?

00:00:09.912 --> 00:00:12.506
The clues were there, scattered across shopper

00:00:12.506 --> 00:00:15.230
activity, product interest and payments.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.625
Could AI do this for Leo?

00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:21.605
Meet Market Metro: a simulated commerce environment

00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:24.685
where AI agents analyze shopper behavior, train

00:00:24.685 --> 00:00:27.539
models and test marketing campaigns.

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:30.694
We measure how their decisions change purchases,

00:00:30.694 --> 00:00:34.525
merchant contribution and PayPal payment outcomes.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.875
The ontology gives that data meaning.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.575
It connects shoppers, products, contacts and payments,

00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:43.475
then maps each prediction to the right

00:00:43.525 --> 00:00:45.100
training examples.

00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:47.975
Recommendations and inquiries stay separate.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.050
Earlier signals can be inputs; later outcomes

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.850
are reserved for labels and evaluation.

00:00:55.283 --> 00:00:58.283
The agent studies demand and available stock,

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:00.633
chooses twenty eight days of history, and

00:01:00.708 --> 00:01:03.608
asks local tools to train two models.

00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:06.483
Its decision summary, tool calls and results

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.708
become checkpoints you can inspect.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.679
Before a campaign launches, the verifier tests

00:01:12.679 --> 00:01:16.428
the candidate models in matching development markets.

00:01:16.428 --> 00:01:19.115
It checks PayPal and merchant outcomes, then

00:01:19.115 --> 00:01:22.298
publishes an eligible tested configuration or keeps

00:01:22.298 --> 00:01:23.925
the current policy.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.875
On day thirty five, a recommendation reaches

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.500
our quiet shopper.

00:01:29.575 --> 00:01:31.450
They buy a ninety six dollar side

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.105
table, and it arrives the next day.

00:01:34.205 --> 00:01:37.405
Fixed rules left that same request unfilled.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.315
PayPal Sandbox captures that exact order.

00:01:41.315 --> 00:01:44.068
Its receipt records ninety six dollars gross,

00:01:44.068 --> 00:01:46.892
the provider fee and merchant proceeds.

00:01:46.892 --> 00:01:49.363
Those records link back to the warehouse,

00:01:49.363 --> 00:01:51.904
so the proof is traceable without counting

00:01:51.904 --> 00:01:53.175
another sale.

00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:58.144
Across the same market, sixteen more purchases

00:01:58.144 --> 00:02:00.053
were completed and kept.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.598
Payment volume grows by two thousand three

00:02:02.598 --> 00:02:04.718
hundred forty five dollars.

00:02:04.718 --> 00:02:07.758
Merchant contribution and modeled PayPal net both

00:02:07.758 --> 00:02:09.384
rise, after AI cost.

00:02:09.384 --> 00:02:11.858
The numbers on screen compare against the

00:02:11.858 --> 00:02:13.342
same fixed rules.

00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:17.426
A G Grid lets judges inspect the

00:02:17.426 --> 00:02:21.236
entire journey: trained models, agent decisions, campaign

00:02:21.236 --> 00:02:24.224
outcomes and the native PayPal receipt.

00:02:24.224 --> 00:02:26.839
Open a checkpoint and follow the evidence

00:02:26.839 --> 00:02:28.483
behind the result.

00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:31.317
Market Metro.

00:02:31.392 --> 00:02:34.192
Agentic commerce that learns, tests its ideas,

00:02:34.282 --> 00:02:35.557
and shows its work.
